# level ids that have changed, and the one-time state rewrite each needs.
#
# the precedent is inprogress: a level's identity changed, so anything keyed
# on the old one is a lie. inprogress solves it by self-invalidating on a
# fingerprint -- it can, because a stale place is worthless. a rename cannot,
# because the state is still CORRECT. it is only filed under a name that no
# longer exists, and thrown away it takes a real boss-fall with it.
#
# format:  <schema>  <old-id>  <new-id>
#
# a <new-id> of - means the level was merged away rather than renumbered.
# that state is not correct under any name, so it is deleted outright --
# ledger lines and card keys both.
#
# applied in file order, once, guarded by $STATE/schema. write rows in
# DESCENDING order so no rename ever collides with a name still in use:
# 30 -> 31 must land before 29 -> 30, or 29 lands on top of the old 30.
#
# a rewrite touches every ledger keyed by level id (passed, placed, briefed)
# and the card keys, which are prefixed <level-id>/. inprogress is dropped
# rather than rewritten: a renumbered level is an edited level, so its
# fingerprint has moved and the place was already void.
#
# 30-root gets no row on purpose. it kept its id but absorbed the level that
# used to sit below it, so every block index in it moved. its card keys are
# now orphans -- held rebuilds keys from the level file, so they count toward
# neither side, and they erode out of the file once they are GRACE_DAYS
# past due with nothing proving them. the boss-fall in
# passed stays: it happened, and passed is never revoked. held will simply
# read false until the merged level is worked back up to tier 2, which is
# exactly right -- it is not the level that was cleared.
1 29-proving-this-machine -
